+# Network local prototype
+
+The local workbench uses synthetic identities, a scripted native Eliza model, and a closed mock provider. It is a review environment. Live identity, model quality, messaging, external sources, operating policy and real product outcomes require separate qualification.
+
+The domain owns membership, source provenance, review and independent consent. The existing relationship stores own factual encounter edges and derived knowledge; the existing approval queue and SQL scheduler own effects and timers. Hosts compose these owners in one SQL transaction. Model capabilities are bound to the authenticated member and private room; identity, age and sharing grants use explicit member controls.
+
+With repository-pinned Bun 1.4.2 and Node 24.15.0, run from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+bun build --production --conditions=eliza-source packages/ui/src/network/entry.tsx --outdir test-results/network-ui/build --entry-naming 'network.[ext]'
+bun --conditions=eliza-source packages/cloud/scripts/network-workbench.ts
+```
+
+Open `http://127.0.0.1:54732/`. The selected local identity is a signed fixture session, not production authentication. The persistent fixture database and generated evidence belong in ignored `test-results/`; do not use real contacts or secrets. The workbench creates no real channel delivery or paid inference.
+
+The `/portal`, `/friend-help` and `/slop-love` routes provide a phone-first member preview. Only the reserved synthetic number `+12025550101` can verify a displayed local code. Auth owns the OTP primitive; the local host owns its closed delivery adapter and revocable fixture sessions. These views open the shared Network conversation, without enrolling another app membership or qualifying private cross-app histories. Real phone signup, approved iMessage delivery and production app isolation require the existing Cloud identity/gateway integration and separate acceptance.
+
+Owning tests exercise the domain on PGlite/PostgreSQL, native graph/effect erasure, scoped conversations and receipts, reviewed outreach, scheduling, matching and deterministic worlds. Run the owning package checks and `bun run verify`; the local qualification and remaining PRD scope are recorded in the generated verification reports.
+
+## Matching vocabulary and evaluation
+
+Keep the current domain and matching types as the vocabulary owners. An intent states a member's objective; `needs` describe requested help and `offers` describe willing contributions. A skill or capability establishes ability, not willingness to help. Tags describe interests or requirements, not consent, trust or eligibility. Profile facts retain confirmation, provenance, scope and time. The domain's `matching` scope projects to the matcher's `network` scope; only `shareable` facts may appear in explanations or external judge inputs.
+
+Fit measures compatibility. Mutual benefit measures what each participant gets from the proposed configuration, including a member's stated wish to contribute. Pair aggregation penalizes a weak side; group aggregation limits how much stronger members can conceal the weakest member. These are ranking heuristics, not probabilities of a worthwhile meeting. Blocks, age, capacity, consent and safety remain hard gates outside scoring. Unknown evidence must not become an inferred offer or permission.
+
+[Synthetic scoring cases](matching/scoring-fixtures.ts) exercise those distinctions and [regression behavior](matching/scoring.test.ts). Their authored expectations are not human labels. Before changing weights or enabling a judge, have two reviewers independently assess a small shared set, with algorithm scores and expected order hidden:
+
+| Review field | Record |
+| --- | --- |
+| Eligibility | Eligible, ineligible or unknown, with the deciding constraint |
+| Benefit for each participant | None, plausible, strong or unknown; cite the member's evidence |
+| Comfort and burden | Any unwanted role, overload or privacy concern |
+| Overall recommendation | Offer, ask for clarification or reject |
+| Evidence | Authorized fact/intent IDs and a short reason |
+
+Keep missing ratings and disagreements explicit. Reconcile vocabulary disagreements before tuning. Use reviewed examples for development and a separately labeled, untouched set for comparison; report coverage, agreement and errors on each side rather than a blended accuracy alone. Human-rated offline quality, synthetic simulations, live provider behavior and actual member outcomes are separate qualifications. Store private ratings and generated comparisons in ignored `test-results/`; never add member conversations to fixtures.
